        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.362,
            0.881,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "From Fig. 14.9, it follows that the curved surface area (CSA) of the cone is equal to"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.402,
            0.69,
            0.496
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_11.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{CSA} = \\frac{1}{2}b_{_1}l + \\frac{1}{2}b_{_2}l + \\cdots + \\frac{1}{2}b_{_n}l = \\frac{1}{2}(b_{_1} + b_{_2} + \\cdots + b_{_n})l \\\\= \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\mathrm{circm} & \\mathrm{reference} \\mathrm{of the base} \\times l \\\\= \\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi r)l = \\pi r l\\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.496,
            0.881,
            0.536
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Here is another way of working out the formula for the curved surface area. We have, from the formula for area of a sector of a circle,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.334,
            0.543,
            0.663,
            0.581
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_13.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{CSA} = \\pi l^{2} \\times \\frac{\\theta^{\\circ}}{360^{\\circ}} = \\pi l \\times l \\times \\frac{\\theta^{\\circ}}{360^{\\circ}}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.581,
            0.881,
            0.62
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us try to express this formula in terms of r and l. For this, note that from the cone, we have,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.386,
            0.624,
            0.609,
            0.645
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_15.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{arc}   length =s=2\\pi r,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.653,
            0.47,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "and from the sector, we have"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.353,
            0.673,
            0.641,
            0.709
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_17.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{arc}\\ \\mathrm{length}=s=\\frac{\\theta^{\\circ}}{360^{\\circ}}\\times2\\pi l."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.711,
            0.448,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Equating these two, we get"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.743,
            0.55,
            0.78
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_19.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac { \\theta ^ { \\circ } } { 3 6 0 ^ { \\circ } } = \\frac { r } { l } ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.788,
            0.677,
            0.807
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "From this, we see that the curved surface area is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.443,
            0.812,
            0.555,
            0.832
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_21.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array} { r } { \\mathrm { C S A } = \\pi r l , } \\end{array}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.84,
            0.881,
            0.881
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "the same formula as earlier. The formula for the total surface area (TSA) (curved portion plus the flat base) is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{TSA} = \\pi r l + \\pi r^{2}"
            }
          ]
        }
